In the following, we focus on binary classification and aim to find a hyperplane that separates the data into two classes. In d-dimensional space, a hyperplane is the set of points \vec{x} that satisfy\beta_0 + \beta_1 x_1 + \beta_2 x_2 + \cdots + \beta_d x_d = \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x} = 0

where we have again absorbed the bias into the weight vector by adding a constant feature x_0 = 1 to the feature vector \vec{x} \in \mathbb{R}^{d+1}. The position of the hyperplane is therefore defined by the weights \vec{\beta}, as illustrated in the interactive figure below. An important property of the hyperplane is that all points \vec{x} on one side satisfy \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x} > 0, while all points on the other side satisfy \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x} < 0.

