Now suppose we have a dataset of N points, represented by \bm{X} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times d+1}, with associated labels y_1, y_2, \cdots, y_N \in \{-1, 1\}. The goal is to find weights \vec{\beta} that define a separating hyperplane, so that points with y_i = 1 lie on one side of the hyperplane (\vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i > 0), while points with y_i = -1 lie on the other side (\vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i < 0). This gives us a classifier: for any new point \vec{x}^*, we predict the label from the sign of \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}^*.

A first observation is that any correctly separating hyperplane satisfiesy_i \, \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i > 0 \quad \forall i = 1, 2, \cdots, N

for every correctly classified point \vec{x}_i.

However, if the data are perfectly separable, there are infinitely many such hyperplanes. A hyperplane can usually be shifted slightly up or down, or rotated, without touching any data point. A robust classifier should therefore not only classify all training or test points correctly, but also maximise the margin, the distance between the hyperplane and the nearest data points.

We now formulate this idea as an optimisation problem, which will allow us to define a loss function for finding the optimal hyperplane. Because we want to maximise the distance from the hyperplane to the nearest data points, one might think that the magnitude of the left-hand side of 

(29) already represents such a distance. This is not true: multiplying \vec{\beta} by a constant factor does not change the hyperplane, since \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x} = 0 still holds, but it does scale the value in 

(29). This is illustrated in the interactive figure below.

With a bit of algebra, one can show that the actual absolute distance between a point \vec{x}_i and the hyperplane is\text{dist}(\vec{x}_i) = \frac{|\vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i|}{\|\vec{\beta}\|_2}

Proof

Define the 

orthogonal projection of \vec{x}_i onto the hyperplane:\vec{x}_{i,p} := \vec{x}_i - \frac{\vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i}{\|\vec{\beta}\|_2^2}\,\vec{\beta}.

Then\vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_{i,p}
= \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i - \frac{\vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i}{\|\vec{\beta}\|_2^2}\,\vec{\beta}^\top\vec{\beta}
= 0,

so \vec{x}_{i,p} actually lies on the hyperplane.

Next, we show that \vec{x}_{i,p} is the point on the hyperplane that is closest to \vec{x}_i. To see this, take any \vec{x} on the hyperplane (\vec{\beta}^\top\vec{x}=0). Using\|\vec{x}_i-\vec{x}\|_2^2
= \|\vec{x}_i-\vec{x}_{i,p}\|_2^2
+ 2\langle \vec{x}_i-\vec{x}_{i,p},\,\vec{x}_{i,p}-\vec{x}\rangle
+ \|\vec{x}_{i,p}-\vec{x}\|_2^2,

the cross term is zero because \vec{x}_i-\vec{x}_{i,p} is parallel to \vec{\beta}, while \vec{x}_{i,p}-\vec{x} is orthogonal to \vec{\beta} (both points are on the hyperplane). Hence\|\vec{x}_i-\vec{x}\|_2^2 \ge \|\vec{x}_i-\vec{x}_{i,p}\|_2^2,

so the minimum is attained at \vec{x}=\vec{x}_{i,p}. Finally,\text{dist}(\vec{x}_i)
= \|\vec{x}_i-\vec{x}_{i,p}\|_2
= \left\|\frac{\vec{\beta}^\top\vec{x}_i}{\|\vec{\beta}\|_2^2}\,\vec{\beta}\right\|_2
= \frac{|\vec{\beta}^\top\vec{x}_i|}{\|\vec{\beta}\|_2}.

Since the label y_i \in \{-1, 1\} already encodes the correct side of the hyperplane, we can define the signed distance of a point \vec{x}_i to the hyperplane as\frac{y_i \, \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i}{\|\vec{\beta}\|_2}

The margin of the hyperplane is determined by the points closest to it, the so-called support vectors. We therefore take the smallest signed distance across all data points and obtain the optimisation problem\max_{\vec{\beta}} \min_{i} \frac{y_i \, \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i}{\|\vec{\beta}\|_2}

The objective in 

(30) contains a nested \max \min expression, which is inconvenient for optimisation algorithms. We can reformulate it by introducing the margin M as an explicit variable:M = \min_{i} \frac{y_i \, \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i}{\|\vec{\beta}\|_2}

The problem is then to maximise only M, which is easier to handle. So far, however, this is only a reformulation. To enforce that M is the minimum signed distance across all points, we require\frac{y_i \, \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i}{\|\vec{\beta}\|_2} \ge M \quad \forall i

If all signed distances are at least M, then the minimum of these distances is also at least M. Maximising M therefore pushes this lower bound as high as possible, which is exactly what we want. Since positive scaling of \vec{\beta} does not change the signed distance, we could instead require y_i \, \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i \ge M for all i and add the constraint \|\vec{\beta}\|_2 = 1, which significantly reduces the search space for the optimal \vec{\beta}.

Epigraph/hypograph formulation of optimisation problems

This is a common trick in robust or worst-case optimisation: replace a nested objective by an auxiliary variable and inequalities. Formally,\max_{\vec{z}}\min_i f_i(\vec{z})
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\max_{\vec{z},t} t \ \text{s.t.}\ f_i(\vec{z})\ge t\ \forall i,

and an analogous reformulation can be used for minima.

Although this optimisation problem is geometrically intuitive, because it maximises a distance to the hyperplane, one more reformulation makes it much cleaner. Recall that we can arbitrarily set the scale of \vec{\beta} without changing the hyperplane. We therefore choose a scaling such thaty_i \, \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i \ge 1 \quad \forall i

If the inequality holds exactly as an equality for at least one point, the geometric margin becomesM = \min_i \frac{y_i \, \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i}{\|\vec{\beta}\|_2} = \frac{1}{\|\vec{\beta}\|_2}

under this optimal scaling. Maximising the margin of the hyperplane is thus equivalent to maximising \|\vec{\beta}\|_2^{-1} or, more conveniently, minimising \frac{1}{2}\|\vec{\beta}\|_2^2. This gives a quadratic and convex optimisation problem, the hard-margin SVM loss, which can be solved efficiently.

