The constraint in 

(31) ensures that all points lie on or outside the margin of the hyperplane. It also implies that all points are classified correctly, as stated in 

(29).

In many real-world problems, however, the data are not linearly separable because points from different classes can overlap. We can allow some points to be misclassified by relaxing the constraint in 

(31) toy_i \, \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i \ge 1 - \xi_i \quad \forall i,

where \xi_i \ge 0 is the slack variable for the i-th point. Rearranging 

(32) shows that the minimal slack variable is \max(0, 1 - y_i \, \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i). Combining this term with the original objective of minimising \frac{1}{2}\|\vec{\beta}\|_2^2 gives the soft-margin or hinge SVM loss:\mathcal{L} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N \max(0, 1 - y_i \, \vec{\beta}^\top \vec{x}_i) + \frac{\lambda}{2} \|\vec{\beta}\|_2^2

Here, \lambda is a regularisation parameter that controls the trade-off between the margin size and the number of misclassified points.

Caution

In the classical SVM formulation, the bias \beta_0 is typically not regularised. Therefore, the second term in 

(33) is not a proper norm. We will ignore this detail in the following for simplicity.

